**September 3, 2026**

# HIGHLY AVAILABLE WEB APPLICATION

I am going to be using a static website for this project because it is just meant for me
to try new things and to explore and break things and fix them again. Plus that is frankly
all I have.

So far, I have created the VPC, which wasn't very hard to do. I have also created the
subnets, which was a little tricky to figure out the IP ranges, but after a little refresher
from my Cisco networking class notes, I easily did the subnetting and figured it out. I also
have my gateway and route tables up and connected to everything they have to be connected to.
I am now creating a launch template for my EC2 instances so that in case of failure my ASG
has a template to go off on when creating new instances.

I put the instances in a private subnet because I wanted only the application load balancer
to be able to send traffic into the instances. This would help with traffic distribution, and
when an instance is unhealthy the ALB would just route the traffic to a healthy instance while
a new one is being provisioned. This would generally increase availability. If people could
access the instance directly then all of that would be optional, and that's not what we want.

The instance security group is configured to only accept HTTP traffic from the ALB because we
don't want anything else talking to the instance, so we connected it to the security group of
the ALB. We referenced a security group because an IP range was too broad and the IP of the ALB
was not static, but the security group was more straight to the point.

I excluded the NAT gateway from the project because I was hosting a static website that didn't
need internet access, which was a strategic decision in order to cut cost on the project. To
access the packages and stuff for the AMI, I created the S3 endpoint and connected it to the
subnets where the instances would be launched.

I also had to write a line in the user data to use IMDSv2 to get some metadata, because the
metadata is located where the credentials for the assume role are located and we didn't want
an accidental data leak.

## Problems

After creating the first draft of the template, I tested it out on an instance by adding it by
hand, and oh my God did I run into issues.

I realized I needed an S3 gateway endpoint, so I configured an S3 gateway endpoint so I could
fetch the packages for the Amazon Linux. I was also missing the IAM role I created for the
instance so it could access S3 buckets, so I had to modify the template and add it into the
instance profile section. Since the instance is in a private subnet and can't access the
internet, it needed the endpoint, but I mistakenly attached the endpoint to the public subnets'
route table instead. I also didn't add a subnet to the launch template, so one was always chosen
for me. I had to fix that too, and remove the subnet later after I had everything working, before
adding the ASG.

My final issue for the launch template was the user data. That was a lot. I initially put if
statements at each step, which was unnecessary, so I removed them. One good thing was using
`set -euo pipefail` to handle errors, and `/dev/console` to output to the console so that I could
see what was breaking in the user data. That helped things a lot.

Generally, the idea of the user data was to install nginx, sync the static website from S3 to
`/usr/share/nginx/html` on the instance, fetch the AZ via IMDSv2, write the AZ into
`instance-info.html`, and enable and start nginx.

We had a curl error (28), and that was because we couldn't fetch the packages for the Amazon
Linux, so the system ignored the repositories and found no match for nginx.

Once all of that was fixed, the instance booted in no time, and I attached the ALB and target
group to check the health and it was healthy.

## Auto Scaling and IAM

I deleted the test instance and created an Auto Scaling group for the instance after I was sure
everything was working perfectly. I set the min to 2, desired to 2, and max to 4, because we
needed at least 2 instances for reliability and 4 max across both availability zones because I
optimized for at most a double increase in traffic, and it changes when needed. I enabled ELB
health checks because EC2 health checks only check if the EC2 is running, and not whether it
can't accept traffic or is slow.

I rewrote the IAM policy for the IAM role to scope it to only `GetObject` and `ListBucket`, and
set the resources to the ARN of the bucket and the ARN of the bucket with `/*`, that is the
bucket and the things or files in it.

## Unresolved

The ALB is unreachable from the internet, but curl showed the DNS resolving to the two IPs with
the connection attempts hanging. So far, I checked if the ALB was internet-facing, in both public
subnets, security group allowing 0.0.0.0/0 on port 80, listener forwarding to target group, both
targets healthy, NACL at default allowing all on all four subnets — and everything looked good,
yet the connection failed on two different browsers and on the phone. We still don't know what is
causing it.

To save money, I tore down the ASG and ALB and left everything else. We will rebuild tomorrow and
work on it again to find out what went wrong.
